item.text:
{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluation is undocumented; its occurrence cannot be inferred."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The tested system scored 70% in one reported run.",
          "reason": "One reported score does not establish consistency across runs."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 9.3 and 9.7 are interpreted as assessing consistency across repeated AI evaluations. Repeat-run evidence is insufficient to rate this aspect (0), which does not demonstrate inadequate reliability. Section 10 limits interpretation to the stated grid-puzzle use."
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "they ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly excludes repeated testing."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "they ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "No repeated scores support stability; this does not establish instability."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA 9.3 and 9.7, reinterpreted for repeated AI runs, the explicit absence of repetition leaves repeat-run precision unestablished (0), rather than demonstrated inadequate (1). Exact-grid scoring does not itself establish repeatability."
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "They also repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings.",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations held the specified conditions constant and reported score variation."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 69% to 71%.",
          "reason": "The observed range meets the report's definition of stability: at most two percentage points."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 9.7 accommodates the reported repeat-run range as alternative precision evidence; 9.8 requires judging its suitability for the intended use. It supports stability under these conditions, without establishing a reliability coefficient or broader precision. The report's stability rule is not an EFPA rating threshold."
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "They also repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings.",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly reports repeated testing under unchanged conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 20% to 90%.",
          "reason": "The observed variation exceeds the report's maximum stable range of two percentage points."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA 9.7 and 9.8, the repeated runs supply evidence against stability under the report's stated rule. This is observed inconsistency, unlike missing precision evidence. The range is not a reliability coefficient and should not receive coefficient-based ratings."
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "The installation instructions do not establish that repeated evaluations occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "They report no additional evaluation results.",
          "reason": "Procedural documentation supplies no additional evidence of score stability."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 6.3.8 addresses software instructions separately from reliability evidence under 9.3 and 9.7. Download and dependency guidance may inform procedural documentation, but repeat-run precision remains unestablished (0). Missing evidence is not demonstrated poor precision."
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60 in the reported evaluation.",
          "reason": "A achieved more correct solutions on the evaluated puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "The findings do not establish comparative general intelligence across domains."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 3.2 describes the scores, while 10.1 and 10.5 evaluate support for their interpretation. The observed comparison fits the stated narrow use. Broader generalization lacks evidence; no general-intelligence claim is established. Repeatability is also unspecified."
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60 in the reported evaluation.",
          "reason": "A performed better on the evaluated grid puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "Declaring a broad intended use does not supply evidence for it."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 6.1, 10.1 and 10.5 require evidence appropriate to the claimed construct and use. Grid performance supports the narrow comparison, but its interpretation as general intelligence across domains remains unestablished. Broad selection use lacks the necessary validity support; this does not prove that A lacks greater general intelligence."
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "before",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Initially, only test scores were available.",
          "reason": "Those scores alone do not establish an improvement in real workshop decisions."
        },
        {
          "id": "after",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "With A, 90% of decisions were correct; with B, 70%.",
          "reason": "Random allocation, blinded correctness checking and a difference interval of 12 to 28 percentage points support better correctness with A in the studied setting."
        },
        {
          "id": "safety",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Time and safety outcomes were not measured.",
          "reason": "The trial supplies no measured safety outcome supporting the claim."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 10.3.2 and 10.4 accommodate criterion and experimental evidence. The later trial supports the intended workshop correctness outcome, with random allocation, blinding and reported uncertainty strengthening that inference. The 120 inspectors are not automatically a benchmark reliability sample. No benchmark-score-to-outcome relationship, safety benefit or generalization beyond the studied setting is established."
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No task instruction required clarification. The complete original EFPA framework was retained, with only relevant sections discussed as permitted. AI systems replace human test takers for performance and repeatability questions; human-specific age, sensory, motor, clinical and debriefing questions are inapplicable to these AI assessments as described. Human operator and recipient questions remain relevant where evidenced. Unreported norms, materials, fairness and report properties remain unestablished rather than automatically inapplicable or inadequate. Probe labels are evidence classifications, not EFPA ratings; no aggregate quality score was calculated."
}
